 &#x0003e;3&#x02009;kg) and adult BMI (&#x0003c;25, 25&#x02013;30 and &#x02a7e;30&#x02009;kg/m<sup>2</sup>) both as categorical covariates. Additionally, the HR estimates for endometrial cancer risk for normal weight women (BMI&#x0003c;25) compared to overweight women (BMI 25&#x02013;30) and obese women (BMI&#x02a7e;30), respectively, provided by the adjusted model are shown. All models were fitted stratified by 5-year birth cohorts utilising attained age as time scale.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6603504f1"/></fig><table-wrap id="tbl1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p content-type="table-title">Baseline characteristics of the women of the study (<italic>n</italic>=38&#x02009;566)</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" border="1" width="85%"><colgroup><col align="left"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/></colgroup><thead valign="bottom"><tr><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">&#x000a0;</th><th colspan="3" align="center" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>Birth weight (kg)</bold></th></tr><tr><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">&#x000a0;</th><th align="center" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold><italic>&#x0003c;</italic>2.5</bold> <bold>(<italic>n=</italic>1975)</bold></th><th align="center" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>2.5&#x02013;3 (<italic>n</italic>=7877)</bold></th><th align="center" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>&#x0003e;3 (<italic>n</italic>=28&#x02009;714)</bold></th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td colspan="4" align="left" valign="top" charoff="50"><italic>BMI at enrolment</italic> (<italic>kg/m</italic><sup>2</sup>)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">&#x02003;Median</td><td align="center" valign="top" charoff="50">23</td><td align="center" valign="top" charoff="50">23</td><td align="center" valign="top" charoff="50">23</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">&#x02003;Quartile 